#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101725} :activity/id #uuid "6bc26614-e494-5e0e-b8e8-0405e49dbaa9" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048578 :topic/id #uuid "aae56e33-e57d-5843-b31c-fcba98a1ca50" :topic/math-academy-id 1163} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101725 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186102033 :tutorial/content "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt} = ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $y=y(t)$ is called a **logistic growth equation.** \n\nNote the following:\n\n- $a$ is a positive constant called the **carrying capacity**.\n\n- $k$ is a positive constant of proportionality.\n\nA typical solution curve $y = y(t)$ to this equation is shown below:\n\n![](images/0f/0f416479bbf72d3b9c1bf48861883e0e77bbc1c1d60d7fa48fb89e6f21c104eb.png)\n\nWe will learn more about the structure of the solution curves in future lessons. But for now, it's helpful to realize that for all $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n0 < y(t) < a.\n$$\n\n \n\nAn example of a logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt} = 10y(50-y).\n$$\n\nHere, $k = 10$ and the carrying capacity is $a=50.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f5791475-4f91-5859-9d5b-1f20283522ea" :tutorial/math-academy-id 982 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a86bd27c-35f7-56a0-9dd4-1415fb4b1ae3" :step/math-academy-id 1925 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101786}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101785 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100942 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100777 :question/id #uuid "a16c2c4f-38c3-5e8c-8962-3ad6c956748a" :question/math-academy-id "e-7321"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a46e688b-117f-5394-bcbb-fbfb7a5b6c7b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048127} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048746}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101197} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101551} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253559} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253560} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253561} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351557} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351558}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Logistic Growth Equation"} :step/id #uuid "fa88498c-b724-5dfa-b5a3-3f4d0dbea254" :step/math-academy-id 27104} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101786 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100943 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100779 :question/id #uuid "29ee4686-7e38-5000-9d89-58e572f84cd7" :question/math-academy-id "e-7404"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a4751337-93bf-5b47-8b3f-32100820b19a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051017}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101549} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101550} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253556} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253557} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253558} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351555} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351556}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Logistic Growth Differential Equation"} :step/id #uuid "257b95d0-a448-51aa-936a-9acc386bda17" :step/math-academy-id 27468 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101800}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101800 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101996 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to create a model of the deer population in a forest. Let's denote the number of deer in the forest at time $t$ as $P(t).$\n\nImagine that we have two factors that influence the size of the deer population:\n\n- Unconstrained, the rate at which the deer population increases is proportional to the size of the deer population (i.e., exponential growth).\n\n- However, as the deer population increases, food resources become more scarce. The forest cannot sustain an arbitrarily large number of deer. Therefore, we must specify the maximum number of deer that the forest can sustain. This maximum number is the forest's carrying capacity, denoted $a,$ as before.\n\nTherefore, we say that the rate of change of the deer population $P(t)$ is **jointly proportional** to *both* the deer population $P$ itself *and* the remaining room for growth, which is $a-P.$ This gives us the logistic growth equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d P}{\\textrm d t} = kP(a-P).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "136097ca-5c5c-587e-918d-decbcb156cbf" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5720 :tutorial/title "Modeling Situations Using a Logistic Growth Equation"} :step/id #uuid "80e2d4b5-6d7a-5649-8978-26fea11f0ae7" :step/math-academy-id 35117 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101785}}] :activity/title "Logistic Growth Models With First-Order ODEs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186101030}]}
